description:
A contributor picks several photos, one of them never stops spinning, and the Save button stays greyed out. There is no error, nothing to retry, and no way forward except starting the sighting over. Scott hit this on 2026-09-08 and reported "trouble uploading pix for Jpod and today, on both cellular and SeaTac WiFi" — two different networks, which is the clue that this is not connectivity.

It is a client-side race in `appendPhotos` (src/sighting-form.ts). The loop takes `index` against a **local** copy of the array:

    const photos = [...this.photos];
    for (const file of files) {
        const index = photos.length;          // index into `photos`
        photos.push({state: 'uploading', file, thumb});
        const thumb = await photoThumbnail(file);   // yields
        const {...} = await readExif(file);         // yields
        uploadPhoto(file, ...).then(url => {
            this.photos = this.photos.toSpliced(index, 1, uploaded);  // index into `this.photos`
        });
    }
    this.photos = photos;                     // only now do the two agree

Between the first `photos.push` and the `this.photos = photos` after the loop, the two arrays disagree about length, and every in-flight callback is indexing the wrong one. If an early upload finishes while a later file is still having its thumbnail and EXIF read, `index` is past the end of `this.photos`, and `toSpliced` past the end **appends instead of replacing** (verified: `['x','y'].toSpliced(5,1,'Z')` is `['x','y','Z']`). The uploaded photo lands as a new entry, and then `this.photos = photos` overwrites the whole array and throws it away. The original entry is still `uploading`, forever.

Reproduced by simulating the exact control flow with two files, 50ms thumbnail, 50ms EXIF, 20ms upload:

    [ {state: 'uploading', file: 'a'},
      {state: 'uploaded',  file: 'b', url: 'https://cdn/b.jpg'} ]
    STUCK 'uploading': a

Note which one is stuck: the **first** photo selected, because its upload completed while the second was still being processed.

Then `canSubmit` closes the trap — it requires zero photos in `uploading` or `failed` state:

    && this.photos.filter(p => p.state === 'failed' || p.state === 'uploading').length === 0;

So the sighting cannot be saved at all.

Two things make this much likelier on a phone, which is where sighters actually are: iPhone photos are large, so `photoThumbnail` and `readExif` are slow and the window is wide; and picking several photos at once is the normal way to do it. A *fast* upload makes it worse, not better — the race needs the upload to win, so good WiFi is as dangerous as bad cellular.

The fix is to stop carrying an index across the await. Identify the photo by reference or by an id minted when it is pushed, and have the callback find it in the current `this.photos`. That also removes the `this.photos[index]?.state === 'removed'` guard's dependence on the index being meaningful.

Watch for the neighbouring hazard while in here: `toSpliced(-1, 1, x)` replaces the LAST element rather than failing, so an index that goes missing corrupts a different photo instead of erroring. That is filed separately.

When `uploadPhoto` rejects, the photo is marked `failed` and that is the end of it:

    }).catch(error => {
        if (this.photos[index]?.state === 'removed')
            return;
        const errored: FailedUploadPhoto = {state: 'failed', file, thumb, error};
        this.photos = this.photos.toSpliced(index, 1, errored);
    });

The caught `error` is stored on the object and never used again. No `reportError`, so no toast and no Sentry event. The contributor gets a thumbnail in a `failed` class and is left to infer what that means and what to do about it; we get nothing at all.

This is precisely the shape decision 031 (docs/decisions/031-surfacing-failures.md) was written to abolish — its opening example is a failure that "reached neither audience", and its argument for coupling the two halves in one `reportError` call is that two separate calls are two chances to do half the job. Here neither call is made.

It has already cost us. Scott struggled with photo uploads across two networks on 2026-09-08 around 15:11 PDT. Sentry has **zero events** in the two hours either side (searched 2026-09-08 21:30–23:30 UTC, errors dataset, no results). Whatever went wrong for him is unrecoverable — we cannot tell a network failure from a storage-policy rejection from a file too large, because nothing was ever recorded. The one report we have exists only because he typed it out by hand, and even that failed to send (salish-1kf).

Fix: call `reportError` from the catch with a sentence naming what the person can do, and keep the `cause` so Sentry gets the underlying error. Worth deciding at the same time whether a failed upload should be retryable from the thumbnail, since "try again" is the obvious response and there is currently no control for it.

Related: salish-8q9 (the race that strands photos in `uploading` — a *different* dead end, reached without any rejection at all).
